pg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Dolphin Tree Sculpture","g":"sights","k":"attraction","la":29.304955,"lo":-94.781343,"d":1261,"ad":"1302 Ball Street"},{"n":"1892 Bishop's Palace","g":"sights","k":"attraction","la":29.303076,"lo":-94.782151,"d":1303,"w":"https://www.galvestonhistory.org/","wp":"en:Bishop's Palace, Galveston","wd":"Q3728690","ad":"1402 Broadway","ds":"The Bishop's Palace, also known as Gresham's Castle, is an ornate 19,082 square feet (1,772.8 m2) Victorian-style house, located on Broadway and 14th Street in the East End Historic District of Galveston, Texas. The building was designed by Nicholas J. Clayton for Walter Gresham and completed in 1892.","wpu":"Bishop's Palace (Galveston, Texas)","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e1/PRINCIPAL_%28SOUTH%29_SIDE_-_Colonel_Walter_Gresham_House%2C_1402_Broadway%2C_Galveston%2C_Galveston_County%2C_TX_HABS_TEX%2C84-GALV%2C26-1.tif/lossy-page1-960px-PRINCIPAL_%28SOUTH%29_SIDE_-_Colonel_Walter_Gresham_House%2C_1402_Broadway%2C_Galveston%2C_Galveston_County%2C_TX_HABS_TEX%2C84-GALV%2C26-1.tif.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Central Cultural Center","g":"sights","k":"museum","la":29.296024,"lo":-94.795205,"d":1568,"ad":"2627 Avenue M"},{"n":"ATSF Roundhouse","g":"sights","k":"ruins","la":29.301666,"lo":-94.811025,"d":1978},{"n":"Grand Galvez Resort","g":"sights","k":"hotel","la":29.292627,"lo":-94.786102,"d":2044,"w":"https://grandgalvez.com/","ad":"2024 Seawall Boulevard","f":["ac"],"wifi":1},{"n":"Porretto Beach","g":"sights","k":"beach","la":29.301028,"lo":-94.77458,"d":2045,"w":"https://www.porrettobeach.com/","ph":"+1 281-808-3431","ad":"824 Avenue M 1/2"},{"n":"Porretto Beach","g":"sights","k":"beach","la":29.301162,"lo":-94.772691,"d":2200},{"n":"Balinese Room","g":"sights","k":"ruins","la":29.29039,"lo":-94.786003,"d":2283,"ds":"The Balinese Room was a famous nightclub in Galveston, Texas, United States built on a pier stretching 600 feet (183 m) from the Galveston Seawall over the waters of the Gulf of Mexico. For decades a dance hall and illegal casino, the Balinese Room was remodeled and reopened in 2001 without a casino. During the early morning hours of September 13, 2008, the structure was destroyed by Hurricane Ike.","wpu":"Balinese Room","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/9d/Balinese_room_galveston.jpg/960px-Balinese_room_galveston.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Shark Attack 5D Theater","g":"sights","k":"attraction","la":29.288884,"lo":-94.790036,"d":2365,"ad":"2412 Seawall Boulevard"},{"n":"Stewart Beach Park","g":"sights","k":"beach","la":29.306093,"lo":-94.76799,"d":2463},{"n":"Gulf Glider","g":"sights","k":"attraction","la":29.287769,"lo":-94.790478,"d":2483},{"n":"Double Decker Carousel","g":"sights","k":"attraction","la":29.287334,"lo":-94.790179,"d":2535},{"n":"Frog Hopper","g":"sights","k":"attraction","la":29.287206,"lo":-94.790303,"d":2548},{"n":"Big Wheelin'","g":"sights","k":"attraction","la":29.287165,"lo":-94.790192,"d":2553},{"n":"Texas Tea Cups","g":"sights","k":"attraction","la":29.287054,"lo":-94.790205,"d":2565},{"n":"Pier Pileup","g":"sights","k":"attraction","la":29.286959,"lo":-94.789926,"d":2579},{"n":"Rock n' Roll","g":"sights","k":"attraction","la":29.28684,"lo":-94.790116,"d":2590},{"n":"Cyclone","g":"sights","k":"attraction","la":29.286653,"lo":-94.790067,"d":2611},{"n":"Galveston Island Historic Pleasure Pier","g":"sights","k":"theme_park","la":29.286632,"lo":-94.789796,"d":2616,"w":"https://www.pleasurepier.com/","wp":"en:Galveston Island Historic Pleasure Pier","wd":"Q3094882","ad":"2501 Seawall Boulevard","ds":"Galveston Island Historic Pleasure Pier is a pleasure pier in Galveston, Texas, United States. Opened in the summer of 2012, it has a roller coaster, 15 rides, carnival games and souvenir shops.","wpu":"Galveston Island Historic Pleasure Pier","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/9e/Pleasure_Pier_in_Galveston%2C_Texas_%28cropped%29.jpg/960px-Pleasure_Pier_in_Galveston%2C_Texas_%28cropped%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Pirate's Plunge","g":"sights","k":"attraction","la":29.286552,"lo":-94.789793,"d":2625},{"n":"Revolution","g":"sights","k":"attraction","la":29.286509,"lo":-94.790019,"d":2628},{"n":"Sea Dragon","g":"sights","k":"attraction","la":29.286407,"lo":-94.789974,"d":2639},{"n":"Sky Shooter","g":"sights","k":"attraction","la":29.286278,"lo":-94.789932,"d":2654},{"n":"Stewart Beach","g":"sights","k":"beach","la":29.30604,"lo":-94.765717,"d":2681},{"n":"Iron Shark","g":"sights","k":"attraction","la":29.285933,"lo":-94.78959,"d":2696,"ds":"Iron Shark is a steel roller coaster at Galveston Island Historic Pleasure Pier. The Gerstlauer Euro-Fighter roller coaster opened to the public on June 1, 2012. Iron Shark was the first Euro-Fighter coaster in Texas.","wpu":"Iron Shark","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/8c/The_Iron_Shark_Euro-Fighter.JPG/960px-The_Iron_Shark_Euro-Fighter.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Texas Star Flyer","g":"sights","k":"attraction","la":29.285501,"lo":-94.78953,"d":2744,"ds":"Texas Star Flyer is a Funtime Star Flyer operating at Galveston Island Historic Pleasure Pier. The ride opened to the public on July 27, 2012. The ride was installed by Ride Entertainment Group, who handles all of Funtime's operations in North America.","wpu":"Texas Star Flyer"},{"n":"Galaxy Wheel","g":"sights","k":"attraction","la":29.285249,"lo":-94.789374,"d":2774},{"n":"USS Stewart","g":"sights","k":"attraction","la":29.33407,"lo":-94.779387,"d":2984,"wp":"en:USS Stewart (DE-238)","wd":"Q7874393","ds":"USS Stewart (DE–238) is an Edsall-class destroyer escort, the third United States Navy ship so named. This ship was named for Rear Admiral Charles Stewart (28 July 1778 – 6 November 1869), who commanded USS Constitution during the War of 1812. Stewart is one of only two preserved destroyer escorts in the U.S. and is the only Edsall-class vessel to be preserved.","wpu":"USS Stewart (DE-238)","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/20/USS_Stewart_%28DE-238%29_underway_at_sea_on_21_November_1943_%2880-G-200282%29.jpg/960px-USS_Stewart_%28DE-238%29_underway_at_sea_on_21_November_1943_%2880-G-200282%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"CVS Pharmacy","g":"essentials","k":"pharmacy","la":29.299016,"lo":-94.79366,"d":1223,"w":"https://www.cvs.com/store-locator/galveston-tx-pharmacies/2425-avenue-j-galveston-tx-77550/storeid=7756"},{"n":"Emergency Room","g":"essentials","k":"hospital","la":29.312249,"lo":-94.778743,"d":1404,"ad":"901 Harborside Drive"},{"n":"Texas Department of Criminal Justice Hospital","g":"essentials","k":"hospital","la":29.31244,"lo":-94.777862,"d":1492,"w":"https://www.utmb.edu/tdcj/","ph":"+1 409 772 7623","ad":"809 Harborside Drive"},{"n":"Shriners Hospitals for Children — Galveston","g":"essentials","k":"hospital","la":29.3095,"lo":-94.777151,"d":1537,"w":"https://www.shrinershospitalsforchildren.org/galveston","ph":"+1 409 770 6600","wp":"en:Shriners Hospitals for Children-Texas","wd":"Q7504101","ad":"815 Market Street","ds":"The Shriners Children's Texas is a 30-bed non-profit pediatric specialty hospital (orthopedic, burn, and other service lines), research, and teaching center located adjacent to the University of Texas Medical Branch in Galveston, Texas, US.","wpu":"Shriners Hospitals for Children-Texas","img":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/0/05/Shriners_Hospital%2C_Galveston.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled"},{"n":"John Sealy Hospital","g":"essentials","k":"hospital","la":29.310796,"lo":-94.777157,"d":1538,"w":"https://www.utmbhealth.com/locations/hospitals-campus