h Foundation","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/fd/John_Sealy_Hospital_UTMB.jpg/960px-John_Sealy_Hospital_UTMB.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"St. Mary Cathedral Basilica (Galveston, Texas)","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":29.304167,"lo":-94.790278,"d":700,"ds":"St. Mary Cathedral Basilica is a Catholic cathedral in Galveston, Texas. It is the primary cathedral of the Archdiocese of Galveston-Houston and the mother church of Catholics in Texas.","wpu":"St. Mary Cathedral Basilica (Galveston, Texas)","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/93/St._Mary%27s_Cathedral_Basilica_-_Galveston_01.jpg/960px-St._Mary%27s_Cathedral_Basilica_-_Galveston_01.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"George Washington Grover House","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":29.308056,"lo":-94.785556,"d":753,"ds":"The George Washington Grover House is a two-story house located at 1520 Market (Avenue D) in the East End Historic District of Galveston, Texas. Built in 1859, the house is one of the oldest brick residences in the city.","wpu":"George Washington Grover House","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/30/Grover_House_Galveston.jpg/960px-Grover_House_Galveston.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"First Presbyterian Church (Galveston, Texas)","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":29.304167,"lo":-94.788611,"d":775,"ds":"First Presbyterian Church is a historic church building at 1903 Church Street in Galveston, Texas.","wpu":"First Presbyterian Church (Galveston, Texas)","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/08/First_Presbyterian_Church_Galveston.jpg/960px-First_Presbyterian_Church_Galveston.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Rosenberg Library","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":29.300833,"lo":-94.792778,"d":1019,"ds":"Rosenberg Library, a public library located in Galveston, Texas, United States, is the oldest continuously operating library in Texas. It serves as headquarters of the Galveston County Library System, and its Executive Director is also the Galveston County Librarian.","wpu":"Rosenberg Library","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/79/The_Rosenberg_Library_--_Galveston.jpg/960px-The_Rosenberg_Library_--_Galveston.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"City Hall (Galveston, Texas)","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":29.300278,"lo":-94.795278,"d":1103,"ds":"The Galveston City Hall is located at 823 Rosenberg Avenue. It is the first of three city halls constructed for Galveston.","wpu":"City Hall (Galveston, Texas)","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5e/Galveston_City_Hall_01.jpg/960px-Galveston_City_Hall_01.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Texas Heroes Monument","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":29.29947,"lo":-94.79431,"d":1177,"ds":"The Texas Heroes Monument is located in Galveston, Texas, and was commissioned by Henry Rosenberg to commemorate the brave people who fought during the Texas Revolution. The monument was built by New England Granite Works using Concord, New Hampshire, granite and bronze. The total cost was $50,000, and it was shipped before June 4, 1899.","wpu":"Texas Heroes Monument","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/50/Texas_Heroes_Monument_%281_of_1%29.jpg/960px-Texas_Heroes_Monument_%281_of_1%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Congregation Beth Jacob (Galveston, Texas)","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":29.2986,"lo":-94.7929,"d":1267,"ds":"Congregation Beth Jacob (Hebrew: בית יעקב) is a Conservative Jewish synagogue located at 2401 Avenue K, Galveston, on Galveston Island, Texas, in the United States. The present synagogue was built by Austrian, Russian and Hungarian immigrants in 1931. It was listed on the National Register of Historic Places in 2024.","wpu":"Congregation Beth Jacob (Galveston, Texas)","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/7b/Temple_Beth_Jacob_Galveston.jpg/960px-Temple_Beth_Jacob_Galveston.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"}],"about":{"ds":"Galveston ( GAL-vis-tən) is a resort city and port on the Gulf Coast of the U.S. state of Texas. It encompasses 211.31 square miles (547.3 km2) on Galveston Island and Pelican Island. As of the 2020 census, the city had a population of 53,695, making it the second-largest municipality in Galveston County, where it also serves as the county seat.","wpu":"Galveston, Texas","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f3/Galveston_Texas_Skyline.jpg/960px-Galveston_Texas_Skyline.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},"wk":"2026-10-18"}